# 8. INVENTORY STOCK AGING – MANDATORY

Inventory stock aging is mandatory.

Use reliable inventory dates from existing records, such as receipt date, batch date or another approved inventory aging date.

Do not invent historical receipt dates.

## 8.1 Configurable aging buckets

Example:

| Aging Bucket | Days |
|---|---|
| Fresh Stock | 0–30 days |
| Normal Stock | 31–90 days |
| Aging Stock | 91–180 days |
| Old Stock | 181–365 days |
| Critical Aging | More than 365 days |

These are sample values. Administrators must configure the actual thresholds.

## 8.2 Configuration scope

- Company.
- Branch.
- Warehouse.
- Item.
- Item Category.
- Batch / Lot, where supported.

## 8.3 Display information

- Stock Quantity.
- Aging Days.
- Aging Bucket.
- Receipt Date.
- Batch / Lot.
- Expiry Date, where applicable.
- Shelf-Life Remaining.
- Warehouse.
- Branch.

Aging indicators:

- Normal.
- Aging.
- Old.
- Critical.
- Expired.

Do not automatically classify old stock as unusable unless the applicable inventory policy requires it.

Expired stock must not be treated as usable.

FIFO/FEFO recommendations must follow existing inventory issue and batch allocation rules.

